          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Does \"with\" require the two terms to sit next to each other in the documentation, or is it an index convention?",
          "answer": "In medical documentation, when the word \"with\" links two conditions, it indicates an association or a causal relationship between them. This usage is based on conventions established in the Alphabetic Index of the ICD-10 coding system. Specifically, it means \"associated with\" or \"due to\" and presumes that relationship without needing explicit confirmation in the documentation.\n\nThis is not strictly a requirement for the terms to be listed next to each other; rather, it is an index convention. The classification assumes the relationship for pairs listed in the index unless there is explicit documentation stating they are not related (source: \"ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection\").\n\nIn summary, \"with\" suggests a recognized link according to the index, regardless of whether the terms appear adjacent in documentation.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
